=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom does not correspond to any actual fault in the network. A thorough inspection of every element named in the helpdesk ticket shows the following:

**RIP process on router0 is fully alive.** The `ripd` daemon (PID 76) is running under `watchfrr` supervision. `show ip rip status` confirms two active RIP neighbors — router1 at 10.16.1.2 (via eth2/WAN) and router5 at 172.20.1.2 (via eth4) — with clean update exchange, zero bad packets, and zero bad routes. The routing table carries five learned RIP routes covering all remote subnets (10.20.1.0/24, 10.50.1.0/24, 172.25.1.0/24, 192.168.15.0/24, 192.168.100.0/24, 192.168.200.0/24).

**Router0's egress interfaces are up.** eth2 (10.16.1.1/24, WAN toward router1) and eth4 (172.20.1.1/24, toward router5) are both LOWER_UP with correct addresses. The VLAN subinterfaces eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24) are also up.

**PC4 and PC5 can reach Location C.** Active pings from PC4 and PC5 to PC11, PC12, and PC13 (192.168.200.x, the switch4/5/6 segment served by router4) all complete with 0% packet loss and normal RTTs (~2 ms). Connectivity outside Location A is fully functional.

**The timeouts to 172.18.50.x / 172.18.51.x are expected.** Those addresses belong to the mswitch2/router6 segment (Location B). Router6 carries an explicit ACL blocking B→A return traffic — a known and intentional security policy in this topology, not an injected fault.

**Root cause:** The premise of ticket #7044 is incorrect; router0's RIP process is healthy, its egress is operational, and Location A users can reach Location C without any issue. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===